    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Neovascular age-related macular degeneration, left eye, active (H35.3221)</content>. New subfoveal choroidal neovascularization with subretinal fluid on OCT (central subfield thickness 418 µm) and acuity reduced to 55 letters. First intravitreal aflibercept 2 mg injection given today without complication. [S]<list><item>Aflibercept 2 mg every four weeks for three loading doses, then treat-and-extend while the macula stays dry.</item><item>Return in four weeks for acuity, OCT, pressure, and the second injection.</item><item>Call the same day for pain, increasing redness, worsening vision, light sensitivity, or a curtain over the vision.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Intermediate dry age-related macular degeneration, right eye (H35.3112)</content>. Large drusen with no neovascularization. This eye now carries most of her reading and driving vision. [S]<list><item>Start the AREDS2 supplement daily (vitamin C, vitamin E, lutein, zeaxanthin, zinc, and copper; no beta-carotene).</item><item>Daily Amsler grid checks of each eye, with a same-day call for any new distortion.</item><item>Dilated examination of the right eye at each retina visit.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Best-corrected visual acuity (ETDRS letters)</td><td>Every injection visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Baseline before treatment: 78 letters right, 55 letters left</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>Optical coherence tomography of the macula</td><td>Every injection visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Both eyes. Left: subretinal fluid, central subfield thickness 418 µm. Both: large drusen</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>Fluorescein angiography or OCT angiography</td><td>At diagnosis of suspected neovascular AMD</td><td>Deferred</td><td>Not performed. OCT and fundus findings were typical, so treatment began the same day without angiography, a departure from the PPP&apos;s diagnostic recommendation. Angiography will be done if the response to loading is poor or the picture becomes atypical</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Intraocular pressure before and after injection</td><td>Every injection visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Before injection 15 mmHg right and 16 mmHg left. After injection the check was by optic nerve perfusion and counting fingers rather than a second pressure reading</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Comprehensive dilated eye examination</td><td>Every 1 to 2 years at 65 or older</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Acuity, pressure, slit-lamp, and dilated fundus examination of both eyes today</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Dilated examination of the fellow (right) eye</td><td>Every injection visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Large drusen; no fluid or hemorrhage</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Intravitreal injection safety</td><td>Every injection visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Povidone-iodine preparation; no complication; first loading dose</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Teaching on warning signs of endophthalmitis and retinal detachment</td><td>Every injection visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Taught today with written instructions in large print</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Daily home Amsler grid, each eye</td><td>Daily</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Reviewed her technique with each eye covered in turn</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>AREDS2 supplement for intermediate AMD</td><td>Daily, long term</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Started today; the formula without beta-carotene</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Functional vision assessment (reading, color, driving, work)</td><td>At diagnosis; again with change</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Trouble with small print and fine color judgment; drives short routes by day</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Low-vision rehabilitation, with home fall-risk review and a driving assessment</td><td>Once central vision is reduced; review as vision changes</td><td>Referred</td><td>Referral sent today</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Depression screen (PHQ-2)</td><td>At follow-up, because vision loss raises the risk of depression; periodically at 65 or older</td><td>Due</td><td>Score 1 two days ago; repeat at the July primary care review</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>Gain at least 10 ETDRS letters in the left eye by the end of the loading phase. In progress; target 2026-05-07 [S]</item><item>Dry macula on OCT (central subfield thickness below 300 um, no fluid) by the end of the loading phase. In progress; target 2026-05-07 [S]</item><item>Check the Amsler grid daily for each eye and report any change the same day. In progress; target 2026-07-02 [S]</item><item>Complete a low-vision rehabilitation evaluation and set up studio lighting and magnification by the July visit. In progress; target 2026-07-02 [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: Age-Related Macular Degeneration Preferred Practice Pattern (2024); CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, cataract and glaucoma monographs (eye-examination items) (2026)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Retina visit in four weeks for acuity, OCT, pressure, and the second injection. Same-day call for warning signs or new distortion in either eye. Next scheduled visit 2026-04-09: four-week retina follow-up with OCT and second injection. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
